  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 250 PM EDT SUN SEP 20 2026\n\nModel suites are in reasonable agreement for much of the long-term \nperiod. The period will start with more of a quasi-zonal upper level \npattern across the Ohio Valley. At the surface, a cold front will be \nin place and quasi-stationary across Kentucky. This feature, along \nwith eventual help from an upper level shortwave will keep the \nweather active through Thursday. The best chances of showers and \nthunderstorms are expected to be Tuesday and Wednesday. There is a \nmarginal risk (1 out of 4) for excessive rainfall Tuesday and \nWednesday. However, it would likely take multiple rounds of heavy \nrainfall to produce any flash flooding issues. This stormier \npattern will help keep afternoon highs much cooler than of late, \nwith highs in the mid 70s to around 80 for Tuesday, and cooler for\nWednesday with highs in the low to mid 70s. Then by the end of \nthe week, we will see an upper-level closed low setup along the \neastern seaboard. This will result in more of a blocking pattern \nacross the CONUS. The good news for the Ohio Valley is surface \nhigh pressure to the north will will usher in much cooler and \ndrier weather for the end of the week into the weekend, as we will\nsee northerly flow at the surface. Afternoon highs will be much \nmore like fall, topping out in the upper 60s to lower 70s.",
